Desktop TUN on macOS registers its DNS server in SCDynamicStore when the
inbound settings carry a dns list, the way sing-tun does on Darwin.

DarwinTun configured the utun interface and its /1..8 routes, yet ignored
options.DNS completely. On Wi-Fi networks where DHCP hands out a router DNS
on the local subnet (such as 192.168.1.1 on a /24 link), the link route on
en0 is more specific than utun's /1..8 routes, so mDNSResponder kept sending
every system DNS query directly to the ISP router outside the tunnel: blocked
domains failed to resolve and Fake IP never saw system lookups.

Now DarwinTun.Start publishes a temporary catch-all DNS resolver entry
(SupplementalMatchDomains [""] with SupplementalMatchOrders [1]) in
SCDynamicStore through CoreFoundation and SystemConfiguration loaded via
libSystem, switches Go's own process resolver off mDNSResponder onto sockets
bound to the physical outbound interface while skipping the TUN DNS address,
and flushes dscacheutil. Because SCDynamicStoreAddTemporaryValue binds the
entry to the store session file descriptor, configd removes it automatically
both on normal Close and if the xray process is killed abruptly.
